    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_76ee64918198eef6966e6fedaa74794321b4f8918a42e7fea56535dba5751815",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Lemma 5.6 certifies the external x-half-lines by composing two stability transformations. First, deforming the finite y-segment to C1/C2 creates exponential y-decay under the small-cubic hypothesis, enabling truncation and local Taylor reduction. Second, the x-half-line is rotated to C3 or C4 according to the sign of the quadratic coefficient. In the branch where the rotated integrand initially grows, the x-integral is written as a full-line Gaussian minus a negative half-line: the correction decays and is reduced through truncation, Taylor expansion, and incomplete-Gamma evaluation, while explicit Gaussian integration converts the bulk term into a short y-integral. Both routes end in the cited quadratic exponential sums handled by Theorem 2.1, so the displayed tail integrals require no additional terminal evaluator. This closure depends on all of the lemma's separation, coefficient, index-range, and smallness hypotheses; it does not establish an unrestricted contour rule.",
