    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3b72d4b75d3ca43138d478a42933ac8a409b41fd996c73bb7b3a78bea313c284",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The stopping cases are constituent branches of the cubic algorithm. Small length permits direct summation, weak quadratic curvature permits Euler-Maclaurin reduction, continued normalized curvature permits quadratic descent, stationary mass is deferred to saddle analysis, and loss of the small-cubic condition routes the terminal high-degree family to precomputed evaluation. Efficiency comes from matching each failed invariant to a different evaluator.",
