    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_93159125f1856139d957ea1734af4fff90f7fb668dfea90c3213849bff44b5bb",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The block length K is a shared design variable: it fixes the number of outer blocks, determines how many Taylor-phase terms remain oscillatory and hence the degree and coefficient ranges of each local sum, and sets the lattice or precomputation size needed to evaluate those sums. The final exponent therefore comes from balancing global block count against coverage of the resulting evaluator parameter space.",
