    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_ab1851ba6f103709c40e28bc964dc79feb1bd8a539d185cc1e867b9da8529cd3",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "When the polynomial degree grows, gridding every higher phase coefficient independently overcounts the true family and breaches the desired precomputation budget. The stationary expansion instead expresses all coefficients above cubic through the shared parameters tau, rho, and gamma; together with the first three phase coefficients this gives a six-parameter function W whose local derivatives support a much sparser interpolation grid and restore the target FFT cost.",
